One way to extract the source of a metaphor from a string in Swift is to use regular expressions. You can define a pattern that matches the metaphor structure and capture the source word.
Here's an example implementation:
```swift
let text = "All the world's a stage, and all the men and women merely players"
let pattern = #"(.+) is a (.+)"#
let regex = try NSRegularExpression(pattern: pattern)
if let match = regex.firstMatch(in: text, range: NSRange(text.startIndex..., in: text)),
   match.numberOfRanges == 3 {
    let sourceRange = match.range(at: 1)
    let source = String(text[Range(sourceRange, in: text)!])
    print(source) // "stage"
}
```
In this example, the pattern `(.+) is a (.+)` matches any string that follows the structure "X is a Y", where X is the source of the metaphor and Y is the target. The parentheses capture the X and Y groups as separate ranges.
