Three pivotal moments during World War I are:
1. Battle of the Marne (September 6-12, 1914, near Paris, France): This battle marked the end of the German advance towards Paris and the beginning of trench warfare. The French and British forces were able to stop the German advance, resulting in a stalemate that lasted for years. One lesser-known factor that contributed to the outcome of this battle was the use of taxis to transport French troops to the front lines.
2. Battle of Jutland (May 31-June 1, 1916, North Sea): This was the largest naval battle of World War I, fought between the British Royal Navy and the German High Seas Fleet. The battle was inconclusive, with both sides claiming victory. However, the German navy was forced to retreat to its home ports and never again challenged the British navy in open battle. One lesser-known factor that contributed to the outcome of this battle was the poor communication and coordination among the British fleet.
3. Treaty of Versailles (June 28, 1919, Versailles, France): This treaty officially ended World War I and imposed heavy penalties on Germany, including the payment of reparations and the loss of territory. The treaty also created the League of Nations, an international organization aimed at preventing future wars. One lesser-known factor that contributed to the outcome of this treaty was the disagreement among the Allied powers over the severity of the penalties to be imposed on Germany.
Here is an implementation of the binary search algorithm for a sorted array in C++:
```
int binarySearch(int arr[], int left, int right, int x) {
    while (left <= right) {
        int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;
        if (arr[mid] == x)
            return mid;
        else if (arr[mid] < x)
            left = mid + 1;
        else
            right = mid - 1;
    }
    return -1;
}
```
This function takes four arguments: the sorted array `arr`, the left and right indices of the subarray to be searched (`left` and `right`), and the value to be searched for (`x`). The function returns the index of `x` in `arr` if it is found, or `-1` if it is not. The function works by repeatedly dividing the subarray in half and comparing the middle element to `x`, then eliminating the half of the subarray that cannot contain `x`. This process continues until `x` is found or the subarray is empty.

