To curate a playlist of songs based on the mood and scenery of a road trip, you can use MATLAB's audio analysis toolbox to extract audio features from a set of songs, and then use machine learning algorithms to classify the songs based on their mood and tempo.
Here is a sample code that demonstrates how to extract audio features and classify songs based on their mood and tempo:
```matlab
% Load the audio files
audio_files = dir('*.mp3');
num_files = length(audio_files);
% Extract audio features
for i = 1:num_files
    [audio, Fs] = audioread(audio_files(i).name);
    features(i,:) = extract_audio_features(audio, Fs);
end
% Normalize the features
features = normalize(features);
% Train a machine learning model to classify the songs
labels = classify_songs(features);
% Create a playlist based on the classification labels
for i = 1:num_files
    if labels(i) == 'happy'
        happy_songs{i} = audio_files(i).name;
    elseif labels(i) == 'relaxed'
        relaxed_songs{i} = audio_files(i).name;
    elseif labels(i) == 'energetic'
        energetic_songs{i} = audio_files(i).name;
    end
end
% Shuffle the songs and create a playlist
playlist = [happy_songs, relaxed_songs, energetic_songs];
playlist = playlist(randperm(length(playlist)));
```
In this code, `extract_audio_features` is a function that extracts audio features such as tempo, spectral centroid, and zero-crossing rate from a given audio file. `normalize` is a function that normalizes the extracted features to have zero mean and unit variance. `classify_songs` is a function that trains a machine learning model to classify the songs based on their mood and tempo.
You can customize the audio features and classification labels based on your preferences and the specific mood and scenery of your road trip.

