Expected conversion rate = (budget for audience A * conversion rate for audience A) + (budget for audience B * conversion rate for audience B) / total budget
We can use this formula to calculate the expected conversion rate for different budget allocations and choose the allocation that maximizes the expected conversion rate.
Here's the C# code that implements this allocation strategy:
```
using System;
class Program {
    static void Main(string[] args) {
        double budget = 1000; // total budget
        double conversionRateA = 0.02; // conversion rate for audience A
        double conversionRateB = 0.05; // conversion rate for audience B
        double maxExpectedConversionRate = 0;
        double budgetForAWithMaxConversionRate = 0;
        // loop through different budget allocations for audience A
        for (double budgetForA = 0; budgetForA <= budget; budgetForA += 10) {
            double budgetForB = budget - budgetForA;
            double expectedConversionRate = (budgetForA * conversionRateA + budgetForB * conversionRateB) / budget;
            if (expectedConversionRate > maxExpectedConversionRate) {
                maxExpectedConversionRate = expectedConversionRate;
                budgetForAWithMaxConversionRate = budgetForA;
            }
        }
        // print the optimal budget allocation
        Console.WriteLine($"Optimal budget allocation: {budgetForAWithMaxConversionRate} for audience A and {budget - budgetForAWithMaxConversionRate} for audience B.");
    }
}
```
This code loops through different budget allocations for audience A (in increments of 10) and calculates the expected conversion rate for each allocation using the formula above. It then chooses the allocation that maximizes the expected conversion rate and prints it to the console.

